explanation":"Each grid contains one 7-object and one 9-object of the same shape. The two objects line up either vertically or horizontally, and pairs of 0 \"posts\" stand along the corridor between them.\n\n**What changes in the demonstrations**\n- **The 7 moves onto the 9.** Every 9 cell becomes 7, and the old 7 cells become background 6. The fact sheet's transitions confirm this: 9→7 and 7→6 in every pair.\n- **Gates turn red.** Some cells on the route between the two objects, which were background, become 2.\n  - Pair 1: the route is columns 6–7 between the 9 at the top and the 7 at the bottom. Rows 3, 5 and 8 have a 0 directly left of the route and a 0 directly right of it, and exactly those rows turn 2.\n  - Pair 2: the route is row 4, x = 2..6. Only x = 3 and x = 5 have a 0 above and a 0 below.\n  - Pair 3: the route is rows 7–8, x = 6..11. Only x = 7 and x = 10 have 0s directly above and below.\n- **The rule for 2.** Cut the corridor between the two objects into slices across the direction of travel. Each slice spans the object's width. If the cell just outside both ends of a slice is 0, that slice is a gate and is filled with 2.\n\n**Applying it to the test**\n- The 7-block is rows 0–3 and the 9-block is rows 9–12, both in columns 10–13. The corridor is rows 4–8.\n- Rows 6 and 8 have 0 at x = 9 and at x = 14, so these two rows become 2 across columns 10–13.\n- Row 11 has 0 posts too, but it lies inside the 9-block, not strictly between the objects. It simply becomes 7 along with the rest of the block.\n- The old 7-block becomes 6.\n\n**Checks**\n- Gate detection was checked against the three demonstrations above; the program is applied to them by the verifier.\n- Teaching stages: the program emits once after moving the 7, then once per gate it paints.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    c7 = [tuple(p) for p in np.argwhere(g == 7)]\n    c9 = [tuple(p) for p in np.argwhere(g == 9)]\n    a = bbox(c7); b = bbox(c9)\n    # move: erase 7, recolour 9 as 7\n    for y, x in c7: out[y, x] = bg\n    for y, x in c9: out[y, x] = 7\n    emit(out, \"Move the 7 object onto the 9 object's position; erase the old 7.\")\n    H, W = g.shape\n    gates = []\n    if a[1] == b[1] and a[3] == b[3]:  # vertical travel\n        x0, x1 = a[1], a[3]\n        lo, hi = (a[2], b[0]) if a[2] < b[0] else (b[2], a[0])\n        for y in range(lo + 1, hi):\n            if x0 - 1 >= 0 and x1 + 1 < W and g[y, x0 - 1] == 0 and g[y, x1 + 1] == 0:\n                gates.append([(y, x) for x in range(x0, x1 + 1)])\n    else:  # horizontal travel\n        y0, y1 = a[0], a[2]\n        lo, hi = (a[3], b[1]) if a[3] < b[1] else (b[3], a[1])\n        for x in range(lo + 1, hi):\n            if y0 - 1 >= 0 and y1 + 1 < H and g[y0 - 1, x] == 0 and g[y1 + 1, x] == 0:\n                gates.append([(y, x) for y in range(y0, y1 + 1)])\n    for cells in gates:\n        for y, x in cells:\n            out[y, x] = 2\n        emit(out, \"Mark a gate the object passed through (flanked by 0 posts) with 2.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each grid contains one 7-object and one 9-object of the same shape."}],"movement":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Move the 7 object onto the 9 object's position; erase the old 7."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The two objects line up either vertically or horizontally, and pairs of 0 \"posts\" stand along the corridor between them."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **The rule for 2.** Cut the corridor between the two objects into slices across the direction of travel."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The corridor is rows 4–8."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"b1e27940e0416d1a6562c6f7cac399955028f29184c2c3973b7b0ce7adf6e4ef","task_sha256":"9efb67c0ffdd653d050e5087272d8d53184c7caa814277f4135d3698530d80b7","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
